WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:04.300
You want a league table for eight schools.

2
00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:06.890
The results are spread across eight websites,

3
00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:08.330
on pages made for people,

4
00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:09.900
not programs.

5
00:00:10.794 --> 00:00:13.134
To collect them, you write a scraper:

6
00:00:13.154 --> 00:00:15.774
a program that downloads those pages

7
00:00:15.794 --> 00:00:17.744
and pulls rows out of them.

8
00:00:18.114 --> 00:00:20.464
The simplest one is a single loop.

9
00:00:21.514 --> 00:00:22.454
For each site:

10
00:00:22.474 --> 00:00:23.894
download the page,

11
00:00:23.914 --> 00:00:25.334
find the results table,

12
00:00:25.354 --> 00:00:26.614
read each row,

13
00:00:26.634 --> 00:00:28.344
and add it to the standings.

14
00:00:28.474 --> 00:00:30.584
On a good day, it works.

15
00:00:30.874 --> 00:00:32.294
Then, one at a time,

16
00:00:32.314 --> 00:00:34.504
five ordinary things go wrong.

17
00:00:36.094 --> 00:00:38.724
First: one site doesn't answer.

18
00:00:38.854 --> 00:00:41.794
The loop waits, gives up, and crashes,

19
00:00:41.814 --> 00:00:43.844
taking the finished sites down with it.

20
00:00:45.642 --> 00:00:49.192
So downloading becomes its own stage: scrape.

21
00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:51.382
It works down a list of addresses,

22
00:00:51.402 --> 00:00:53.222
checks whether each site answered,

23
00:00:53.242 --> 00:00:55.112
waits, and tries again.

24
00:00:55.402 --> 00:00:58.022
A silent site is now scrape's to deal with,

25
00:00:58.042 --> 00:00:59.512
instead of a crash.

26
00:01:00.042 --> 00:01:02.982
What it should do about one is a real decision.

27
00:01:03.002 --> 00:01:04.312
Hold on to that.

28
00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.460
Second: a month later, you find a bug.

29
00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.740
Your code had home and away scores backwards.

30
00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:14.930
You fix it and go back for the pages,

31
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:16.370
but the sites have changed,

32
00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:18.980
and some of last month's pages are gone.

33
00:01:20.678 --> 00:01:22.228
That brings in store.

34
00:01:22.438 --> 00:01:24.818
Save every page exactly as it arrived,

35
00:01:24.838 --> 00:01:26.868
before any of your code reads it.

36
00:01:27.078 --> 00:01:28.628
Now a bug is cheap.

37
00:01:28.758 --> 00:01:31.458
Fix the code and run it again on the saved pages,

38
00:01:31.478 --> 00:01:33.668
without asking the web for anything.

39
00:01:36.334 --> 00:01:38.044
Third, and quieter:

40
00:01:38.174 --> 00:01:39.754
a site is redesigned,

41
00:01:39.774 --> 00:01:42.764
and a ticket table now sits above the results.

42
00:01:43.054 --> 00:01:45.434
The loop reads the first table it finds,

43
00:01:45.454 --> 00:01:46.474
the wrong one,

44
00:01:46.494 --> 00:01:48.204
and nothing complains.

45
00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:53.780
Reading the page becomes its own stage: parse.

46
00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:56.450
It finds the schedule by its label,

47
00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:58.100
not its position,

48
00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:01.060
and hands on rows in one fixed shape:

49
00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:04.180
date, opponent, result.

50
00:02:05.962 --> 00:02:08.392
Fourth: put the rows together.

51
00:02:08.602 --> 00:02:12.632
A match between A and B is listed on A's page,

52
00:02:12.682 --> 00:02:14.392
and again on B's.

53
00:02:14.442 --> 00:02:15.992
Add up every row,

54
00:02:16.042 --> 00:02:18.472
and every match counts twice.

55
00:02:21.054 --> 00:02:24.364
That forces build, where rows become matches.

56
00:02:24.494 --> 00:02:26.994
It decides which copy of each match to keep,

57
00:02:27.014 --> 00:02:29.084
and only then works out the standings.

58
00:02:29.294 --> 00:02:32.204
Which copy it keeps is another quiet choice.

59
00:02:32.254 --> 00:02:33.884
Hold on to that one, too.

60
00:02:35.756 --> 00:02:38.776
Fifth: the run ends and hands you a table.

61
00:02:39.386 --> 00:02:41.046
Did every site answer?

62
00:02:41.066 --> 00:02:42.696
How many rows came in?

63
00:02:42.906 --> 00:02:44.216
Were there errors?

64
00:02:44.586 --> 00:02:46.056
The table can't say.

65
00:02:48.322 --> 00:02:49.952
That is report's job.

66
00:02:50.162 --> 00:02:52.062
It writes down what the run did:

67
00:02:52.082 --> 00:02:53.582
the pages it fetched,

68
00:02:53.602 --> 00:02:54.862
the rows it found,

69
00:02:54.882 --> 00:02:56.592
and the errors it hit.

70
00:02:57.990 --> 00:02:59.650
Line the five stages up,

71
00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:02.100
and one loop has become a pipeline.

72
00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:05.380
Scrape hands pages to store.

73
00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:08.900
Store hands saved pages to parse.

74
00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.940
Parse hands rows to build.

75
00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:13.890
Build makes the table,

76
00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:16.420
and hands its counts to report.

77
00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:19.090
Each stage can be checked on its own,

78
00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:21.540
and run again without starting over.

79
00:03:24.106 --> 00:03:25.976
Now read that report again.

80
00:03:26.346 --> 00:03:27.766
Eight pages fetched,

81
00:03:27.786 --> 00:03:29.606
one hundred and four rows,

82
00:03:29.626 --> 00:03:30.856
no errors.

83
00:03:31.066 --> 00:03:33.576
Every line is about what the run did.

84
00:03:33.786 --> 00:03:36.376
Not one says what the run should have found.

85
00:03:36.586 --> 00:03:39.736
That gap is what this module is about.

